{
	"nodes":[
		{
			"id":"ad8fe09560e0bbf0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Dissolution",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-490,
			"y":-940,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"ec718b65c5b6e76a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Ions in solution",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-150,
			"y":-940,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"08ffa8532785dec6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Possible Reactions",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":190,
			"y":-940,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"17e08c32d4d48725",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Net Ionic Equation",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":530,
			"y":-940,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"2dd508951a616fb8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Dissociation==: When an ionic compound dissolves, the polar water molecules pull the crystal lattice apart, freeing the individual ions to move independently. **Strong bases dissociate.**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":200,
			"y":-100,
			"width":420,
			"height":205
		},
		{
			"id":"8e6bb370db1b8f4b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Weak Electrolyte== only partially dissociates. The solution weakly conducts electricity.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-637,
			"y":240,
			"width":285,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"e2bd777027825c26",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Strong Electrolyte== dissociates completely into ions. The solution is highly conductive.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1040,
			"y":240,
			"width":325,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"83884004f22ad898",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Non-Electrolyte== dissolves into individual molecules but creates zero ions. The solution does not conduct electricity.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-275,
			"y":240,
			"width":315,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"5d767b0fde08f2d7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"An ==Electrolyte== is a substance that produces an electrically conducting solution when dissolved in a polar solvent (such as water). The substance itself does not conduct electricity in its pure, solid form; it only becomes an electrolyte when it breaks down into mobile ions that can carry an electrical charge through the fluid.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-760,
			"y":-420,
			"width":530,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"ace7071c28954bcd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Classification of Electrolytes",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-715,
			"y":-90,
			"width":440,
			"height":70
		},
		{
			"id":"581b0f709973dd3d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For some reason, Dean teaches in Chemistry class that the strong electrolytes ionizes greater than 5%, and weak electrolyte less than 5%. \"The 5% Rule\", from what internet search says, is to make calculations easier - but I have no idea what kind of calculations it's talking about.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1450,
			"y":-55,
			"width":665,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"a8fa6768db379ad6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Not all electrolytes are acids or bases (e.g. NaCl). However, every soluble acid and base is an electrolyte because their fundamental chemical behavior relies on generating ions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1412,
			"y":-395,
			"width":460,
			"height":170
		},
		{
			"id":"1ea17351e456c057",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The 8 Strong Bases==\n\n**\"Nasty Little Kids Rubs Cats, Sr. Castro Bear\"**\n$NaOH$, $LiOH$, $KOH$, $RbOH$, $CsOH$, $Sr(OH)2$, $Ca(OH)_{2}$, $Ba(OH)2$\n\nNote: strong bases are neutral. However, they split into hydroxide ions ($OH^-$) and metal cations (+1 or +2).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1375,
			"y":520,
			"width":670,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"75cda574578f1f5c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The 7 Strong Acids==\n\n**\"SO I Brought NO Clean CLOthes\"**\n$HSO_{4}$, $HI$, $HBr$, $HNO_{3}$, $HCl$, $HClO_{4}$, $HClO_{3}$\n\nNote: strong acids are neutral. However, they split into hydrogen ions ($H^+$) and anions (-1 or -2).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1224,
			"y":860,
			"width":545,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"4a309c62b468ea7a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"How does solubility rule help with predicting precipitation reactions?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1660,
			"y":240,
			"width":460,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"43e03fe12818d796",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A single complex substance breaks down into simpler parts.\n\n$$\nAB \\to A + B\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-2910,
			"width":640,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"488d3147100db918",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A more active element kicks out and replaces an element in a compound.\n\n$$\nA + BC \\to AC + B\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-2695,
			"width":640,
			"height":130
		},
		{
			"id":"f3ae53ccc32b8bc6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Two ionic compounds swap partners (often forming a solid precipitate).\n\n$$\nAB + CD \\to AD + CB\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-2485,
			"width":640,
			"height":105
		},
		{
			"id":"821761f898e05053",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A carbon fuel burns in oxygen to produce carbon dioxide, water, and heat.\n\n$$\n\\text{Fuel} + O_{2} \\to CO_{2} + H_{2}O\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-2285,
			"width":640,
			"height":125
		},
		{
			"id":"3d40eae4d9b3972c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Empirical Formula==: The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a substance (e.g. Methane $CH_{2}O$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3580,
			"y":-2920,
			"width":480,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"f78749e06fb79d9f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Chemical Formulas\nDescribes a single substance",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3275,
			"y":-3220,
			"width":560,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"23960220f28cc341",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Molecular Formula==: the actual number of atoms of each element in a single molecule (e.g. Glucose $C_{6}H_{12}O_{6}$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2910,
			"y":-2920,
			"width":450,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"cd95d7235cc9ee4b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Two or more simple substances combine to form a complex product.\n\n$$\nA + B \\to AB\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-3140,
			"width":640,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"36ec5e1f5b2bf5a4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Oxidation-Reduction (Redox)== reactions are chemical processes where electrons move from one substance to another.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2090,
			"y":-2405,
			"width":600,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"c9c4a7b57415a927",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Oxidation== is the loss of electrons. It causes an increase in an atom's oxidation number.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2880,
			"y":-2480,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"a6e2ac4f9dbee76e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Reduction== is the gain of electrons. It causes a decrease in an atom's oxidation number.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2880,
			"y":-2320,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"f32552921cdc2dee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The Reducing Agent== donates electrons and gets oxidized in the prodcess.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3440,
			"y":-2472,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"8e5ea376a875d501",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The  Oxidizing Agent== accepts electrons and gets reduced in the process.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3440,
			"y":-2310,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"1e2039ecae5594e7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Double Replacement is NEVER redox.**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2175,
			"y":-2200,
			"width":430,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"68464ac372c37ff6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Balancing Redox Equations",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3040,
			"y":-2000,
			"width":730,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"47ba2721ca97da23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Acid-Base Neutralization== is a specific sub-type of a double replacement reaction where the acid gives away an $H^+$ ion and the base accepts it.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3100,
			"y":-2855,
			"width":460,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"02af2ec0d0f37e66",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"They neutralize each other and produce an insoluble solid, a gas, or a weak electrolyte (water).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3740,
			"y":-2855,
			"width":420,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"4835426db7bf9bec",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Example: Mixing $HCl(aq)$ and $NaOH(aq)$\n\nMolecular Equation\n$$\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) \\to NaCl(aq) + H_{2}O(l)\n$$\n\nComplete Ionic Equation\n$$\nH^+ + Cl^- + Na^+ + OH^- \\to Na^+ + Cl^- + H_{2}O(l)\n$$\n\nNet Ionic Equation\n$$\nH^+(aq) + OH^-(aq) \\to H_{2}O(l)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":4350,
			"y":-2979,
			"width":760,
			"height":368
		},
		{
			"id":"90daecf80d4a468d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Acid-Base Neutralization** and **Oxidation-Reduction (Redox)** are the fundamental chemical mechanisms driving the reaction.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2420,
			"y":-3175,
			"width":540,
			"height":155
		},
		{
			"id":"04f5e09532825ccc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Category A: By Particle Detail (Aqueous Chemistry)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1120,
			"y":-4140,
			"width":480,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"06cfe216185d4e92",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Molecular Equation==: Shows reactants and products as whole, neutral compounds.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-360,
			"y":-4340,
			"width":480,
			"height":130
		},
		{
			"id":"9b2af59afd6a809a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Net Ionic Equation==: Cancels out spectator ions to target only the particles actually forming new bonds.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-360,
			"y":-3880,
			"width":480,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"94ab3ece50b28804",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Examples",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":350,
			"y":-4105,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"b189ff4b65a83f96",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Mixing Silver Nitrate and Sodium Chloride\n\n**Molecular Equation**\n$$\nAgNO_{3}(aq) + NaCl(aq) \\to AgCl(s) + NaNO_{3}(aq)\n$$\n\n**Complete Ionic Equation**\n$$\nAg^+(aq) + NO_{3}^-(aq) + Na^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq) \\to AgCl(s) + Na^+(aq) + NO_{3}^-(aq)\n$$\n\n**Net Ionic Equation**\n$$\nAg^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq) \\to AgCl(s)\n$$\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-4730,
			"width":760,
			"height":390
		},
		{
			"id":"ad9cf37a86a7d486",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Mixing Hydrochloric Acid and Sodium Hydroxide\n\nMolecular Equation\n$$\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) \\to H_{2}O(l) + NaCl(aq)\n$$\n\nComplete Ionic Equation\n$$\nH^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq) + Na^+(aq) + OH^-(aq) \\to H_{2}O(l) + Na^+(aq) + Cl^+(aq)\n$$\n\nNet Ionic Equation\n$$\nH^+(aq) + OH^-(aq) \\to H_{2}O(l)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-4250,
			"width":760,
			"height":350
		},
		{
			"id":"ac5953e597a78a35",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Mixing Hydrochloric Acid and Calcium Carbonate\n\nMolecular Equation\n$$\nCaCO_{3}(s) + 2HCl(aq) \\to CaCl_{2}(aq) + H_{2}O(l) + CO_{2}(g)\n$$\n\nComplete Ionic Equation\n$$\nCaCO_{3}(s) + 2H^+(aq) + 2Cl^-(aq) \\to Ca^{2+}(aq) + 2Cl^-(aq) + H_{2}O(l) + CO_{2}(g)\n$$\n\nNet Ionic Equation\n$$\nCaCO_{3}(s) + 2H^+(aq) \\to Ca^{2+}(aq) + H_{2}O(l) + CO_{2}(g)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":920,
			"y":-3790,
			"width":760,
			"height":360
		},
		{
			"id":"ac8288ea7a7b5774",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"When an acid mixes with a carbonate, the overall process is broken down into a two-step reaction mechanism:\n1) Double-Displacement (+ Acid-Base Neutralization)\n2) Decomposition\n\nFor example, when hydrochloric acid and calcium carbonate mixes together:\n1) They form a soluble salt Calcium Chloride $CaCl_{2}$ and Carbonic Acid $H_{2}CO_{3}$. Carbonic acid is formed by carbonate ions accepting protons.\n2) Carbonic Acid decomposes immediately to $H_{2}O$ and $CO_{2}$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1860,
			"y":-3790,
			"width":560,
			"height":380
		},
		{
			"id":"6ae6bd13696e336f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Complete Ionic Equation==: Breaks down all soluble ionic compounds into their separate floating ions. Only the aqueous solutions break down into ions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-360,
			"y":-4117,
			"width":480,
			"height":145
		},
		{
			"id":"5601c3da83da7903",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Subscript is changed to a coefficient unless it's polyatomic ion.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":350,
			"y":-3660,
			"width":360,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"e5f69a4d2177939c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Weak electrolytes are generally kept together.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":355,
			"y":-3520,
			"width":350,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"d55bce3fd52d457d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Synthesis (Combination)==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-3110,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"31b818490da8e324",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Decomposition==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-2885,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"3e23b17a52e3fe44",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Single Replacement/Displacement==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-2670,
			"width":340,
			"height":70
		},
		{
			"id":"275ed6b75224f9ed",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Combustion==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-2252,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"ec5d20ef35235691",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Double Replacement==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-2462,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"728475b602b1d177",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Ionization==: Highly polar covalent compounds react chemically with water, and their molecules split apart to form brand new ions. **Strong acids ionize.**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":700,
			"y":-95,
			"width":440,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"b5851fe3bcbe8f34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"e.g. HCl -> H + Cl",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":790,
			"y":180,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"4111497d03e10374",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"e.g. NaCl -> Na + Cl",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":280,
			"y":180,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"cf253588205895a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"When an electrolyte enters a solvent, it undergoes one of two processes to create free-moving ions - **Dissociation** and **Ionization**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":390,
			"y":-370,
			"width":540,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"1e9b0bc1b30e34e1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A metal higher on the **Activity Series** can replace a metal lower on the activity series in a compound.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2100,
			"y":-1860,
			"width":540,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"aaa9e7fc7bd60f4d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For example, given this\n$$\nZn(s) + CuSO_{4}(aq) \\to ZnSO_{4}(aq) + Cu(s)\n$$\n\nNet ionic:\n$$\nZn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \\to Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2030,
			"y":-1640,
			"width":680,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"2725ca7170819f62",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## 6 Types of Reactions",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-520,
			"y":-2705,
			"width":640,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"1582bfbd7b185268",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Category B: By Reaction Patterns (How Substances Behave)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1160,
			"y":-2695,
			"width":460,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"0483acda446d5f79",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Chemical Equations\nDescribes substances interacting",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1940,
			"y":-3460,
			"width":560,
			"height":160
		}
	],
	"edges":[
